From 8f25e3f43209429b9e941eed04446b6c8b9e1883 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daedalao <daedalao@hotmail.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 20:00:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] linux/ppc: position independent code; c-shared,
 c-archive and plugin build modes

32-bit PowerPC has no PC-relative addressing, and the port so far used
absolute addressing everywhere (PIE executables rely on text relocations).
Shared libraries need real position independent code:

- R13 (the SysV small data base, unused by Go and by glibc outside
  setjmp) is the module base register in -shared/-dynlink code. Every
  function that references a symbol starts with PICBASE (a new pseudo
  instruction): mflr r12; bcl 20,31,$+4; mflr r13; mtlr r12; addis/addi
  r13 with an R_ADDRPOWER_PCREL pair to runtime.text. Nothing is required
  of callers, so functions may be entered from C, signal handlers or
  another module. Compiled non-leaf functions keep R13 at 4(R1) and reload
  it after calls; assembly functions recompute it after calls.
- Symbol references become base-relative addis/D-form pairs
  (R_ADDRPOWER_TOCREL): resolved by the Go linker when linking internally,
  and emitted as R_PPC_REL16_HA/LO with a compensating addend for the
  host linker otherwise. Linker trampolines use the same addressing.
- TLS uses the initial-exec model through a Go-owned word holding the
  thread pointer offset (R_TLS_IE -> R_PPC_TPREL32).
- -dynlink (plugins) reaches symbols that may live in another module
  through Go-owned address words (TOC.sym, reusing the AIX rewriting),
  typed as no-pointer data. Calls bind through the host linker's dynamic
  REL24 relocations.
- runtime: _rt0_ppc_linux_lib constructor, rt0_lib_go, asmcgocall_no_g,
  addmoduledata; crosscall1/2 preserve R13 and CR. The linker generates
  go:link.addmoduledata for plugins and keeps what runtime.addmoduledata
  references reachable.
- -buildmode=pie keeps the absolute scheme (cmd/go passes no -shared for
  ppc); c-archive, c-shared and plugin are enabled for linux/ppc.

Verified on a PowerBook G4: a c-shared library and a c-archive called from
C (goroutines, maps, callbacks, recover), and a plugin loaded by a Go host
(cross-module calls into the host runtime, host access to plugin variables).

